  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will build to a 500mb height of 590dam and be \nthe predominant feature through Friday.  This should result in high \ntemperatures in the low to mid 80s with low temperatures in the \nupper 50s to around 60 for the latter half of this week.  The \npattern will start to change for late Saturday through early next \nweek due to an upper-level trough that is expected to dig in across \nthe Desert Southwest. Model consensus shows the upper-level trough \nand low-level moisture axis both slowing down and remaining further \nwest over New Mexico until Sunday. As a result, chances for rain on \nSaturday and Saturday night have decreased substantially compared to \nearlier forecasts. However, mid-level moisture should start to \nincrease on Saturday, leading to increasing clouds.  Clouds and \nmoisture will continue to increase for Sunday and Monday as the \nupper trough opens up and shifts eastward.  At this point, low \n(<25%) rain chances are expected for Sunday and Monday with slightly \ncooler temperatures in the 70s. Models are also in fairly good \nagreement on a cold front moving in from the northern Plains by \nMonday night into Tuesday. This would increase rain chances more \nsubstantially this time period, but at this time the model spread of \nrainfall amounts do not look heavy.  As will all weather systems 5-7 \ndays out, this potential outcome could easily change over the next \nfew days and will be monitored.",
